  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the critical path method?",
      "a": "A scheduling method that models a project as linked activities with durations, finds the earliest and latest times for each, and identifies the longest chain, the critical path, which sets the finish date.",
      "asks": [
        "CPM",
        "What is CPM in project management?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do you do a forward pass?",
      "a": "Start the first activities at time zero; each early finish is early start plus duration; an activity's early start is the latest early finish of all activities before it.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a forward pass and backward pass?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is float in CPM?",
      "a": "Total float is how long an activity can be delayed without delaying the project: late start minus early start. Activity C here has 1 day, drainage E has 8.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you calculate float in CPM?",
        "Total float"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do you find the critical path?",
      "a": "Do the forward and backward passes; activities with zero total float are critical. Here A, B, D and F, taking 19 days, form the critical path.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you find the critical path of a project?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is crashing a project?",
      "a": "Shortening the project by adding resources to critical activities, choosing those with the lowest extra cost per day saved, while checking that new critical paths do not appear.",
      "asks": [
        "Project crashing"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The critical path method was developed in 1957 by Morgan R. Walker of DuPont and James E. Kelley Jr. of Remington Rand.",
    "PERT, a related method with uncertain durations, was developed for the US Navy Polaris missile programme in 1958."
  ],
